1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,373
Tuesday morning.

2
00:00:01,423 --> 00:00:02,223
Ines.

3
00:00:05,792 --> 00:00:10,113
The problem: developers usually hear
about standards in a review meeting,

4
00:00:10,163 --> 00:00:11,970
days after they wrote the code.

5
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:16,760
This part shows Ines finding out on her
own, with the fix next to every failure.

6
00:00:17,924 --> 00:00:18,833
Tuesday morning.

7
00:00:18,883 --> 00:00:22,851
Normally Ines would hear about Rob's
standards in a review meeting,

8
00:00:22,901 --> 00:00:24,650
days after she wrote the code.

9
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:29,447
Instead the platform worked it out overnight,
and the answer is waiting for her,

10
00:00:29,497 --> 00:00:30,526
with instructions.

11
00:00:35,037 --> 00:00:35,859
Ines logs in.

12
00:00:35,909 --> 00:00:41,091
The home page tells her three APIs are
non-compliant, and offers to show them.

13
00:00:45,145 --> 00:00:50,742
The API list now carries a compliance
summary for the whole estate: three APIs,

14
00:00:50,792 --> 00:00:51,815
none compliant.

15
00:00:52,961 --> 00:00:55,132
Every API shows its own status,

16
00:00:55,182 --> 00:00:59,001
so anyone can see the state
of the estate at a glance.

17
00:00:59,051 --> 00:01:00,577
Hers is non-compliant.

18
00:01:10,885 --> 00:01:14,608
On her API's Governance tab is
the verdict: non-compliant,

19
00:01:14,658 --> 00:01:17,665
with twenty-five errors
and seventeen warnings.

20
00:01:18,828 --> 00:01:22,162
Each ruleset gets its own
card with a compliance score.

21
00:01:22,212 --> 00:01:26,038
Rob's platform standards give her
twenty-five out of a hundred.

22
00:01:26,088 --> 00:01:28,560
The OWASP baseline gives her forty-three.

23
00:01:35,641 --> 00:01:37,856
She filters the list to Rob's rules.

24
00:01:37,906 --> 00:01:42,888
Each row names the rule that failed and
the problem it causes if it isn't fixed:

25
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:46,411
no authentication, no rate
limit, missing operation IDs.

26
00:01:50,932 --> 00:01:54,682
Opening an issue shows the
rule, the ruleset it came from,

27
00:01:54,732 --> 00:01:56,975
and the part of the API it affects.

28
00:01:58,118 --> 00:02:02,202
And here is how to fix it, in Rob's
own words, written yesterday.

29
00:02:02,252 --> 00:02:07,290
This is the difference between being told
no, and being told what to do instead.

30
00:02:11,358 --> 00:02:14,582
Ines lives in the terminal,
so she checks there too.

31
00:02:14,632 --> 00:02:19,492
The same verdict is recorded on the
Kubernetes resource itself: status warned,

32
00:02:19,542 --> 00:02:22,892
twenty-three errors, and the
first few of them inline.

33
00:02:24,076 --> 00:02:27,329
Every evaluation is also
recorded as a Kubernetes event,

34
00:02:27,379 --> 00:02:32,284
so the alerting and audit tools the team
already runs pick it up with no extra work.

35
00:02:33,445 --> 00:02:36,215
Twenty-five errors sounds
like a bad morning.

36
00:02:36,265 --> 00:02:41,229
It is Tuesday, she has until Friday, and
every one of them tells her what to do.
